  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nMid level ridging across the Gulf states will help keep warm and\nmuggy conditions present across the CWA on Wednesday. While a\nshortwave is poised to bring showers/storms Wednesday evening (see\nmore below), cloud cover looks limited enough early in the day that\nwe should heat up into the low 90s thanks to some strong southerly\nflow coupled with the high to the south. The fly in the ointment here\nis that heat indices near the Tri-State do splotchily tickle low end\nAdvisory criteria in the low 100s. However, the signal isn't strong \nenough either spatially or temporally to warrant a full Advisory, so \nwill continue to mention in the HWO and social media messaging. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nA short wave will track from the northern Plains across the upper\nGreat Lakes, pulling a cold front into the area Wednesday night.\nMoisture out ahead of the front appears to be limited, but some \ninstability does develop, with around 2000 J/kg of SBCAPE being \nrealized by Wednesday afternoon. The strongest bulk shear remains\nnorthwest of I-71 during the afternoon hours, so some strong to \nsevere storms would be possible during that time frame, especially \nfor the northwestern portion of our CWA, where deeper shear may aid \nin storm organization. With that being said, a southwesterly LLJ\nramps up during the evening and into the overnight hours, and could\nhelp support a stronger storm or two for areas near the Tri-State\nduring the evening time frame. Main concern for severe continues to\nbe damaging wind gusts, though would not rule out an isolated spin up\nnearer to where the deeper shear is north of I-70 in central Ohio. \n\nAs the front slowly sags down, it looks to take on a southwest to\nnortheast orientation and be very slow to progress. PWATs along the \nfront are forecast to be around 200% of normal. As any robust CAPE \ngets pinched off and a a southwesterly overnight LLJ ramps up, the \nthreat for repeated rounds of storms (training) increases, especially\nfor areas along and south of I-70.\n\nInterestingly, today's afternoon and evening guidance has come in lighter\non the overnight QPF footprint and instead paints higher totals \nduring Thursday early morning and daytime hours. Newest guidance \npicks up on a shortwave disturbance to our west looks to ride along \nthe stalled boundary, increasing moisture content through the column \non Thursday day. This, combined with an increase in diurnal heating \nand mid-level forcing continues the heavy rain and training \nthunderstorm threat across much of our area, possibly with more \nimpactful rainfall than the overnight timeframe. Will be curious to\nsee if subsequent runs continue this trend. \n\nAll of this activity will push southeast of the area, \nalong with the front, on Thursday evening and most of the area will \ndry out on Friday (some lingering shower chances exist along the Ohio\nRiver region, closer to where the front lingers across KY). Long \nrange guidance hints at a weak/poorly organized mid level wave moving\nup from the Gulf through eastern Ohio/Kentucky as we head into the \nweekend, though details are limited at this point.",
